# The app has one route (ADR-0005: the Wheel's place is not addressable), so any
# other path is a typo or a stale link. Send it to the Wheel rather than a 404.
#
# This is a 302, not a `200` rewrite. A rewrite would serve the prerendered `/`
# HTML while the URL bar still read `/whatever`, and the client router — which
# knows only `/` — would find no matching route on hydration and render its own
# 404 over the top. Redirecting fixes the URL first, so the app boots on the
# route it was built for.
#
# The rules above the catch-all are what make it survivable. Workers Assets does
# not look for a matching asset first — "redirects are always followed,
# regardless of whether or not an asset matches the incoming request" — so a bare
# `/*  /  302` also matches `/` itself and every hashed file under `/_app/`, and
# the site answers nothing but a redirect loop. A `200` proxy back to the asset's
# own path is how you say "serve this, do not redirect it": a proxy destination
# is resolved as an asset lookup and is not re-matched against this file, so it
# cannot loop. Top-most rule wins, and static rules must precede dynamic ones.
#
# The exemption must proxy each path to *itself*. Writing the root one as
# `/  /index.html  200` looks more honest and silently does nothing: Cloudflare
# resolves `/index.html` back to `/`, reads the rule as a cycle, and drops it —
# after which the catch-all matches `/` and the loop is back. Verified against
# `wrangler dev`, which reproduces both behaviours.
#
# Anything new in `static/` needs its own exemption here, or the catch-all will
# swallow it. `/index.html` is deliberately left to the catch-all, which folds it
# onto the canonical `/`.
/            /              200
/robots.txt  /robots.txt    200
/_app/*      /_app/:splat   200
/*           /              302
